{"id": "32319", "revid": "5037355", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=32319", "title": "Shamakhi", "text": "Shamakhi (Shemakha, \u015eamax\u0131) is a town in Azerbaijan's Baku Region, capital of the Shamakhi Rayon.\nUnderstand.\nShemakha - is the more classic international way of spelling (originated from Russian pronunciation). Shamakhi is the formal Azeri internal spelling.\nHistory.\nShamakhi was one of the ancient cities of the east. It became famous as the home of many prominent Azerbaijani philosophers, architects and scientists, such as Khagani, Nasimi, Bakuvi, Sabir, Shirvani, Hajibababekov. The town used to be capital of Medieval state of Shirvan and played one of the major roles in the region until its destruction in 1717 by Dagestanian semi-states. Under the Russian Empire Shamakhi was the capital of Shemakha Governorate, but the earthquake of 1859 devastately hurt the city. Then capital moved to Baku and the Governorate was renamed. Shemakhi is mentioned by the great Russian poet A.S. Pushkin in \"The Tale of the Golden Cockerel\" (\"Give me to you a damsel, Shemahan queen.\").\nSeismicity.\nThe city is located in the most seismic area of the Caucasus and was hit by powerful earthquakes in 1191 and 1859, which was so destructive that the capital of Shirvan was transferred to Baku twice. In 1872, the earthquake triggered emigration to Baku, where oil production had started in industrial proportions.\nThe 1667 Shamakhi earthquake is considered to have been the worst with a death toll of 80,000, with one-third of the city collapsed, according to the Persian merchants' reports. The last catastrophic earthquake was recorded in 1902, which destroyed the 10th-century Juma Mosque. Shamakhi is near the boundary of three plates.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nYou can reach the town by following the M4 highway, the northern Baku-Tbilisi connection along the Caucasus Mountains.\nGet around.\nShamakhi can easily be explored on foot."}
{"id": "32320", "revid": "2391", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=32320", "title": "Shamali Plain", "text": "The Shamali Plain (or Shomali Plain) is north of Kabul. Shamali, meaning 'windy' or 'northern,' is a green plain which has produced a lot of the food for central Afghanistan. From Kabul it extends north through Charikar, Parvan province to Jabal os Saraj.\nUnderstand.\nThe Shamali Plain was the front line between warring factions during Afghanistan's many conflicts. The local population suffered particularly badly under the Taliban and during the various wars.\nGet in.\nThe main road heading north from Kabul passes through the plain.\nSee.\nThere are various viewpoints, with some of the best views from Jabal Saraj.\nEat.\nLocal produce such as grapes and other fruit are very good.\nStay safe.\nThe area has been the scene of major battles during the various wars. Get advice on possibly mined roads and other hazards."}
{"id": "32325", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=32325", "title": "Shamokin", "text": "Shamokin is a small city in the Alleghenies and Susquehanna Valley of east-central Pennsylvania.\nUnderstand.\nThe name \"Shamokin\" is a Delaware Indian word for \"Eel Creek.\" At its peak, the community had a population of 50,000 (including surrounding Coal Township). Traditionally, the main industries were coal mining and textile mills, and their remnants can still be noticed today. The eastern and western parts of the town are actually part of Coal Township, but the boundary lines are mostly indistinguishable. Today, the population is less than 20,000. People are generally friendly and willing to help visitors.\nGet in.\nBy car, the closest interstate highways are 80 (to the north) and 81 (to the southeast). State Routes 61, 901, 125, and 225 all provide access from each direction.\nUptown Vans offers a route from Paterson and New York City.\nGet around.\nThe best way to get around is by car, but the Lower Anthracite Transportation System (LATS) offers infrequent bus transportation and taxis are available if called ahead."}
{"id": "32327", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=32327", "title": "Shamokin Dam", "text": "Shamokin Dam is a small town in Snyder County, Pennsylvania.\nUnderstand.\nThe town lies approximately 50 minutes north of Harrisburg and 50 minutes south of Williamsport in the central part of the state, along the Susquehanna River, Shamokin Dam is approximately 157 miles (three hours drive) from Philadelphia, three hours from New York, four hours from Washington-Baltimore and four hours (212 miles) from Pittsburgh. State College, home of the Pennsylvania State University, is 1 hour 31 minutes drive along PA Rte 45.\nGet in.\nBy boat.\nShamokin Dam lies along the Susquehanna River Water Trail with access at the Sunbury Fabridam in the Adam T. Bower State Park.\nBy bike.\nThe BicyclePa Route J passes through Shamokin Dam.\nNarrow and/or unpaved shoulders along most side streets make biking around town (off the highway) challenging. Route 11 from Shamokin Dam to Northumberland is lovely with a view of the river. There is, however, heavy truck traffic along this route.\nGet around.\nBy train.\nShamokin Dam is approximately 60 miles (45 minutes) north of Pennsylvania's capital city of Harrisburg. That city has a station on Amtrak's Keystone and Pennsylvanian train routes. Shamokin Dam is a 45 minute drive north on Rte 322 then Rtes 11&amp;15.\nBy car.\nShamokin Dam is accessible from Interstate 80, which runs east-west north of the area. Pennsylvania routes 11&amp;15 merge north of Harrisburg and serve as the main transportation artery for central Pennsylvania. Most of Shamokin Dam's shopping district lies along Rtes 11 and 15. South of Shamokin Dam, 11/15 South connects with Rte 322, Interstates 81, 83 and eventually, the Pennsylvania Turnpike (Harrisburg Area) west to Pittsburgh and east to Philadelphia.\nOne area challenge for drivers is that on Fridays and holiday weekends the traffic backs up and slows on Rte 11. Early morning travel can avoid the problem. Additionally, Rte 11 along the river towards Northumberland, is often closed when there is flooding. Those seeking to access Route 80 can use Route 15 to connect by traveling a few miles north of Lewisburg. This drive is a four lane highway with some red lights in the Lewisburg area.\nGo next.\nFor additional exploration of heritage, culture, dining and shopping in the Central Susquehanna Valley, be sure to check out Lewisburg, PA and Sunbury, PA. Amish farms are nearby on Routes 11 &amp; 15 Port Trevorton and along Route 45. Look for the hand made quilts blowing in the breeze along the highway. They frequently have roadside stands featuring farm products including fresh baked goods, eggs, and vegetables.\nSports fans will find Hershey Bears ice hockey in Hershey PA about 1 hour away. Red Barons baseball and Wilkes-Barre - Scranton Penguins hockey are all within a two-hour drive. State College, home of Penn State University, is also about 45 minutes away and offers additional cultural, sports (Nittany Lions), and entertainment options. PSU's Bryce Jordon Center offers many top name entertainment acts, as well as college sports. Longer distances will take you to the Atlantic coastline."}
{"id": "32328", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=32328", "title": "Shamrock", "text": "Shamrock is a town in the Texas Panhandle.\nGet in.\nShamrock is on Interstate 40 about 20 miles east of McLean and 85 miles east of Amarillo. Route 83 also connects it with Wellington and Childress to the south, and Wheeler and Canadian to the north.\nGet around.\nAs in other towns in the area, you'll want to have a car.\nBuy.\nShamrock has a number of craft and antique stores."}
{"id": "32330", "revid": "2441217", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=32330", "title": "Shandong", "text": "Shandong (\u5c71\u4e1c Sh\u0101nd\u014dng) is a province in China. Also referred to as Chiloo (\u9f50\u9c81 Qilu), The Giant State (Shandong people are known to be the tallest in the country), and the hometown of Confucius. \nThe name means east of the mountains. Shandong is a coastal province situated by the Bohai Sea to the north and the Yellow Sea to the south. The Yellow River runs through the province.\nMain attractions are Qufu (the birthplace of Confucius), Mount Tai (holy mountain in Chinese belief, the most climbed mountain in China), Qingdao (nice port city with beaches and German buildings) and Yantai (holiday city with lots of European buildings). The capital, Jinan (a city of springs where water and willows are famous), is renowned for its artesian springs and serves as a transport hub for the region. \nUnderstand.\nClimate.\nShandong has a temperate climate: humid continental (K\u00f6ppen Dwa); it is bordering humid subtropical (Cwa under the K\u00f6ppen climate classification) in the south. Generally, summers are hot (typical max 35\u00b0C) and rainy (except for eastern parts of Jiaodong Peninsula (typical max 28\u00b0C) and Mount Tai (typical max 20\u00b0C)), while winters are cold and dry. Average temperatures are \u22129 to 1 \u00b0C (16 to 34 \u00b0F) in January and 18 to 28 \u00b0C (64 to 82 \u00b0F) in July. Annual precipitation is 550 to 950 mm (22 to 37 in), the vast majority of which occurs during summer, due to monsoonal influences.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nThere are a few major international airports including Jinan Yaoqiang Airport, Qingdao Jiaodong Airport, Yantai Penglai Airport, and Weihai Dashuipo Airport.\nBy bus.\nLong-distance buses are another useful way to travel around Shandong, especially for reaching smaller cities and towns that are not conveniently connected by high-speed rail. Most major cities have long-distance bus stations with services to other parts of the province. Buses can also be a convenient option for travelling to some smaller tourist destinations.\nSee.\nMount Tai: A World Heritage Site, enjoy sunrise views from the summit and experience the majestic scenery of the \"Most Dominant of the Five Mountains.\"\nConfucius Temple, Cemetery, and Mansion in Qufu: The hometown of Confucius, learn about Confucian culture and history.\nDo.\nClimb Mount Tai: Choose to hike or take the cable car for a pilgrimage-like experience.\nQingdao Beach Activities: In summer, enjoy swimming, beach volleyball, and strolling along the coast.\nEat.\nShandong pancakes: a local breakfast specialty, thin, crispy and delicious.\nShandong cuisine classics: sweet and sour carp, nine-turn large intestine, Dezhou braised chicken.\nDrink.\nTsingtao Beer: Famous both at home and abroad, best enjoyed chilled in summer.\nSpring water tea: Jinan spring water can be used directly to make tea, which has a refreshing taste.\nStay safe.\nWhen climbing Mount Tai or other scenic areas, pay attention to safety railings and wear comfortable hiking shoes.\nBe mindful of personal belongings, especially at popular scenic spots and beaches.\nGo next.\nJiangsu Province: Such as Nanjing and Suzhou, experience the ancient cities and gardens of Jiangnan.\nHenan Province: Such as Luoyang and Shaolin Temple, explore the history and culture of the Central Plains.\nMountain-sea route: Qingdao \u2192 Weihai \u2192 Yantai, drive along the coast or take a train to enjoy the coastal scenery."}
